---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import ComponentGrid from "../../../components/ComponentGrid.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import { url } from "../../../lib/urls";
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Components"
  description="Small, composable pieces with native behavior. Copy a complete example, understand its semantics, then adapt it to your project."
  section="Build with Lineframe"
  source="website/src/pages/docs/components/index.astro"
>
  <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
    <p>
      Components are CSS classes, not framework-specific wrappers. Start with the element that fits
      the task and add a base class. Modifiers such as
      <code>lf-button--solid</code> layer on top of that base.
    </p>
  </div>
  <CodeBlock
    id="component-composition"
    code={`<a class="lf-button lf-button--solid" href="${url("docs/installation/")}">
  Read the installation guide
</a>`}
    label="Base class + modifier + native element"
  />
  <ComponentGrid />
  <div class="lf-prose docs-prose">
    <h2 id="how-to-use-examples">How these examples work</h2>
    <p>
      Most previews and their displayed code come from the same local HTML string. The preview’s
      outer frame, copy control, and spacing belong to this website—not the component you copy.
    </p>
    <p>
      Demo forms use a small, documentation-only handler for validation feedback. Those <code
        >data-demo-*</code
      > attributes are not library APIs. Your application must supply its own submission and validation
      logic.
    </p>
    <h2 id="what-needs-javascript">What needs JavaScript?</h2>
    <p>
      Layout, buttons, fields, alerts, badges, tables, disclosures, and pagination do not need
      library JavaScript. Theme preference and a generated article table of contents are the two
      optional
      <a href={url("docs/javascript/")}>JavaScript behaviors</a>.
    </p>
    <h2 id="keep-the-semantics">Keep the semantics when you customize</h2>
    <p>
      A style does not provide a role, a name, or behavior by itself.
      <code>aria-disabled</code> does not disable a link, a red border does not explain an error, and
      an alert class does not imply that a screen reader should interrupt. Each focused guide covers the
      responsibilities for that component.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
